------------------------------------ Integrate with a UI library -----------------------------------

- REPLACE CUSTOM ALERTS WITH NG-BOOTSTRAP COMPONENTS AND ADD PAGINATED FINISHED RACES.

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite "Advanced components" chapter

- 😶‍🌫️I’ve got to be honest with you: what we did with our Alert…​ has already been done by others
    before :). 🔥Indeed, ng-bootstrap is a popular component library which offers some very cool
    components, and among them: NgbAlert.
    Take the time to look at the source code
    (https://github.com/ng-bootstrap/ng-bootstrap/blob/master/src/alert/alert.ts) doesn’t it look
    familiar? :) So let’s use this component instead of ours.


1️⃣ Install ng-bootstrap:

        npm install --save-exact @ng-bootstrap/ng-bootstrap@20.0.0 @popperjs/core@2.11.8
    
    or
        npm install @ng-bootstrap/ng-bootstrap @popperjs/core
    
    🔥🔥As ng-bootstrap offers some internationalization support, we need to add the Angular package
    for i18n, introduced in Angular 9 (even if we won’t be using it, it is just for compilation
    purpose).
    Run:

        ng add @angular/localize
    
    OK, we’re ready to use it!



2️⃣ Replace every <pr-alert></pr-alert> by a <ngb-alert></ngb-alert> (and replace the import
    by NgbAlert).
    When that’s done, you can remove the Alert files (don’t forget to clean the imports in other
    files too).



3️⃣ Now that we have this library at hand, maybe we can use some of the components offered. Let’s
    add a pagination to the FinishedRaces. I don’t know if you noticed, but this tab displays a
    lot of races! Let’s add a pagination component(https://ng-bootstrap.github.io/#/components/pagination/overview)
    at the top of template.
    🔥We are going to store the selected page in a query parameter, so the URL will be updated
    when the user clicks on a new page. For example, if the user clicks on page 3, the URL will be
    updated to /races/finished?page=3.

    🔥We are going to refactor the component to have a ViewModel object containing all the data
    we need to display. Add an interface ViewModel in finished-races.ts

    🔥Then, in the FinishedRaces class, add a vm computed signal containing ViewModel | undefined
    (and replace the races field by a resource).

    Keep the resource returned by raceService.list('FINISHED') in a races field. 🔥Add another
    signal for the current page using an input named page, with 1 as the default value and
    transformed with numberAttribute, so the router can bind the page query parameter to it.
    You can use the following code:

        readonly page = input(1, { transform: (value: unknown) => numberAttribute(value, 1) });
    
    🔥Then create a computed() producing a new ViewModel object, with the page extracted from the
    query parameter, the total as the length of the array returned by the resource, and races as
    the races to display (you can use races.slice((page - 1) * 10, page * 10) to display the 10
    races corresponding to the page). If the resource does not have a value yet, the computed
    should return undefined.
    finished-races.ts:

     👉 interface ViewModel {
            page: number;
            total: number;
            races: Array<RaceModel>;
        }
        // omitted
        export class FinishedRaces {
            private readonly router = inject(Router);
            protected readonly races = inject(RaceService).list('FINISHED');
        👉 readonly page = input(1, { transform: (value: unknown) => numberAttribute(value, 1) }); 

        👉 readonly vm = computed<ViewModel | undefined>(() => {
                if(!this.races.hasValue()) {
                    return undefined;
                }
                const allRaces = this.races.value();
                const page = this.page();
                return {
                    total: allRaces.length,
                    page,
                    races: allRaces.slice((page - 1) * 10, page * 10)
                }
            })

            protected changePage(page: number): void {
                this.router.navigate([], { queryParams: { page } });
            }
        }



4️⃣ Let’s use our new vm signal in the template (wrap the whole template with an if to check that
    vm is defined). Add the pagination component at the top of the template:

     👉 @if (vm(); as vm) {
        👉 <ngb-pagination [collectionSize]="vm.total" [page]="vm.page" (pageChange)="changePage($event)" size="lg" />
            @for (race of vm.races; track race.id) {
                <pr-race [raceModel]="race" />
            }
        } @else if (races.isLoading()) {
            <div role="status">Loading.</div>
        } @else if (races.error()) {
            <ngb-alert [dismissible]="false" type="danger">An error occurred while loading races.</ngb-alert>
        }



5️⃣ Keep the loading and error states in the template too: - display Loading. while the resource is
    loading - display an ngb-alert containing An error occurred while loading races. if loading
    fails



6️⃣ Add a changePage method to the component that navigates to the current route but with a page
    query parameter with the new page number (🔥that will be emitted by the pageChange event). You
    should now have a nice pagination on your tab, and the page should change when you click!

        private readonly router = inject(Router);
        protected changePage(page: number): void {
            this.router.navigate([], { queryParams: { page } });
        }



